.TH SEND-RUN-CONTROL-COMMAND.SH 1 "2026-09-10" "mu2edaq-shifter-tools" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
send-run-control-command.sh \- send a run-control state transition (not implemented)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B send-run-control-command.sh
[\fB\-c\fR \fIconfig\fR]
[\fB\-z\fR \fIpartition\fR]
\fB\-e\fR \fIenvironment\fR
[\fB\-H\fR \fIhost\fR]
[\fB\-n\fR]
\fIcommand\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B send-run-control-command.sh
is intended to drive otsdaq run-control state transitions from the
command line, by sending a UDP datagram to the gateway supervisor of
one DAQ environment.
.SH STATUS
.B This command is not implemented.
.PP
Its option handling, configuration resolution and target-port
calculation are complete and covered by the test suite, but the wire
format of the otsdaq run-control UDP message has not been transcribed,
so no datagram is ever sent. Invoking it prints the resolved target
and exits 3.
.PP
Drive state transitions from the otsdaq web GUI instead, reachable
through the port forwards opened by
.BR daq-tunnels.sh (1).
.PP
The implementation notes at the end of the script record what remains
to be done: read the UDP listener in the otsdaq gateway supervisor,
transcribe the payload, and send it to the already-resolved host and
port.
.SH OPTIONS
Configuration precedence is command line, then environment variable,
then .env file, then config file, then built-in default.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " " \fIFILE\fR
Operations YAML to read. Environment: \fBMU2EDAQ_CONFIG\fR.
.TP
.BR \-z ", " \-\-partition " " \fINAME\fR
Partition owning the environment. Environment:
\fBMU2EDAQ_PARTITION\fR. Default: \fIpartition_0\fR.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-environment " " \fINAME\fR
Target environment. Required. Environment:
\fBMU2EDAQ_ENVIRONMENT\fR.
.TP
.BR \-H ", " \-\-host " " \fIHOST\fR
Gateway host to address. Environment: \fBMU2EDAQ_RC_HOST\fR. Default:
\fIlocalhost\fR, which is what a forwarded port looks like.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Print the resolved target and exit 0 without attempting to send.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a usage summary and exit.
.SH COMMANDS
.B init configure start stop pause resume halt
.PP
Any other verb is rejected before the target is resolved.
.SH TARGET PORT
The port is the partition's \fBots_port_offset\fR plus the
environment's \fBport_offset\fR, which is how ots itself derives it.
The same number is available as
.PP
.RS
.nf
daq-read-config -z \fIpartition\fR -e \fIenvironment\fR gateway-port
.fi
.RE
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SEND_RUN_CONTROL_COMMAND_SH_OPTS
Extra options, prepended to the command line.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
A dry run completed.
.TP
.B 1
The configuration could not be read, or the port could not be
computed.
.TP
.B 2
No environment or command was given, or an option was not recognised.
.TP
.B 3
The command is not implemented. This is the status of every real
invocation.
.SH EXAMPLES
Check which host and port a transition would target:
.PP
.RS
.nf
send-run-control-command.sh -z partition_0 -e tracker -n start
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR daq-tunnels.sh (1),
.BR daq-read-config (1),
.BR daq-status.sh (1)
